 ## Background
-The term "direct volume rendering" was coined by Marc Levoy in his 1988 paper "Display of Surfaces from Volume Data" [S6, unofficial]. Levoy defined the volume rendering pipeline, which remains an influential framework [S6, unofficial]. The technique was originally designed to display weak or fuzzy surfaces with smooth silhouettes and few aliasing artifacts [S6, unofficial].
+Direct volume ray casting was pioneered by Marc Levoy in his seminal 1988 paper "Display of Surfaces from Volume Data" [S8, S9, unofficial]. Levoy coined the term and defined the standard visualization pipeline (classification, shading, compositing) [S8, unofficial]. The method was initially developed for CPU-based rendering and later adapted for GPU acceleration in the early 2000s (e.g., Roettger et al. 2003, Stegmaier et al. 2004) as fragment shader capabilities improved [S5, unofficial]. It remains the foundational technique for interactive volume visualization in medical imaging and scientific visualization [S4, S9, S26, unofficial].
 
